1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,786
Light can change an amino-chain, a peptide.

2
00:00:02,786 --> 00:00:05,169
But not every chain reacts the same way.

3
00:00:05,169 --> 00:00:07,239
It depends on which links it is made of.

4
00:00:07,239 --> 00:00:14,687
In lab studies, a few links are the usual suspects: tryptophan, tyrosine, phenylalanine, and cysteine.

5
00:00:14,687 --> 00:00:17,213
When light hits them, they can oxidize.

6
00:00:17,213 --> 00:00:21,093
A chain without those links has far fewer places for that to happen.

7
00:00:21,093 --> 00:00:23,111
But the links do not act alone.

8
00:00:23,111 --> 00:00:24,401
Their neighbors matter.

9
00:00:24,401 --> 00:00:29,700
In one study, a histidine link next to tryptophan was oxidized under light.

10
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:33,919
The same peptide, with the tryptophan swapped out, did not show it.

11
00:00:33,919 --> 00:00:35,703
So the sequence decides.

12
00:00:35,703 --> 00:00:38,580
Two chains, same light, different result.

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:44,101
That is why some labels say protect from light, and why no single rule covers every chain.

14
00:00:44,101 --> 00:00:46,028
Follow for more peptide math.

